# Neel

Neel is a Nim library for HTML/JS GUI apps (inspired by Python's Eel). It runs
its own threaded HTTP/WebSocket server on `127.0.0.1`, launches a
Chromium-family browser in app mode, and bridges Nim procs and JS functions with
return values in both directions. Neel 2.0 is released and lives on `master`.

The current work is **serve mode** (`startApp(serve = true, ...)`): no browser,
a persistent server for one user, reached through a tunnel or reverse proxy. It
is built on the `feature-headless` branch and merges into `master` only when the
user asks.

## Source of truth

- `SPEC.md` at the repo root holds every settled decision, the behavior, the
  build steps (section 13), and the running log of findings, deviations, and
  gotchas (section 16). Read all of it before any work.
- The decisions in `SPEC.md` are settled. Do not reopen them in passing; if one
  turns out to be wrong or unimplementable, stop and ask.
- If the spec conflicts with the code or is unclear, stop and ask; do not
  silently pick one.
- `README.md` and `docs/GUIDE.md` describe the shipped 2.0 behavior and the
  wire protocol ("The `neel.js` API").

## Session workflow

Each session implements exactly one build step from `SPEC.md` section 13.

1. Read `SPEC.md` fully, then the modules the step touches.
2. Implement only that step. Do not start the next step or refactor unrelated
   modules.
3. Run `nimble test` and `nim c src/neel.nim` before finishing. Note which Nim
   compiled it (`nim -v`, and Nimble's "using ... for compilation" line; they
   can differ).
4. In the step's entry: set `**Status:** done` (or `partial` plus what is
   missing), add deviations to **Notes** in one or two lines each, and list the
   manual checks the user still has to run.
5. Record anything later steps need in `SPEC.md` section 16: dated findings,
   deviations (and edit the spec section they change, so the spec stays true),
   and new gotchas. Chat is not a record.
6. Do not commit unless the user asks. There is no CI; the user verifies on
   macOS, Windows, and Linux by hand and records results in section 13.8.

## Hard constraints

- Nim >= 2.2.12 (enforced by `neel.nimble` and a compile-time check in
  `src/neel.nim`; older allocators crash on cross-thread frees). ORC and
  threads are on by default; do not add flags that assume otherwise.
- Zero external Nimble dependencies. Standard library only.
- The server binds `127.0.0.1` only, in every mode.
- Local mode (the default) must keep behaving as it does today. The only
  accepted difference is the displaced close code (`SPEC.md` section 11).
- Never build shell command strings for launching processes; use
  `startProcess` with an args array.
- Shipped code and doc comments never cite `SPEC.md`, step numbers, or the log;
  they state the constraint itself.
- Keep anything written purely for ad-hoc verification out of the repo (use
  `/tmp`).

## Layout

```
src/neel.nim          public surface (startApp, runApp, routes, re-exports)
src/neel/*.nim        internal modules; each header states its responsibility
src/neel/neel.js      browser-side shim, staticRead and rendered by frontend.nim
tests/                unit and integration tests, run by `nimble test`
examples/             runnable example apps
docs/GUIDE.md         the full user guide
SPEC.md               serve mode spec, build steps, and log
```
